Single-user migration history for Jon's Gradebook.

Rules for writing migrations
----------------------------

1. Never import the ORM models (app.db.models) and never call
   Base.metadata.create_all() from a migration. A migration must describe a fixed
   change; if it reads the live models, what it creates changes every time a model
   changes. Revision 20260421_0001 did this and broke fresh installs (issue #1).
   tests/test_migrations.py enforces this rule.

2. Write explicit DDL with op.create_table / op.add_column / op.create_index.
   `alembic revision --autogenerate -m "..."` against an up-to-date database is a
   good starting point; review the output before committing.

3. Data-seeding SQL must supply every NOT NULL column explicitly. Databases created
   before revision 20261022_0018 may lack the server defaults a fresh install has,
   so do not rely on server defaults in INSERT statements.

4. Guard DDL with the inspector (has_table, get_columns, get_indexes) when a change
   may already exist on some installs, and make downgrade() reverse what upgrade()
   created, including PostgreSQL enum types (DROP TYPE IF EXISTS ...).

5. Keep the models and the migrated schema in sync. After editing a model, add a
   migration and confirm `alembic check` reports "No new upgrade operations detected".

Verifying migrations
--------------------

    cd backend
    # Fresh install, drift check, and downgrade/upgrade round trip on a throwaway DB:
    TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://USER:PASS@localhost:5432/postgres pytest tests/test_migrations.py

    # Against your own development database:
    alembic upgrade head
    alembic check
